cards.answer:
The fundamental difference is what the variable actually contains. A value type variable stores the actual data, while a reference type variable stores a reference to an object that contains the data. Assigning a value type copies the value itself; assigning a reference type copies the reference, so multiple variables can refer to the same object.
```
int x = 10;
int y = x;
y = 20;
Console.WriteLine(x); // 10
Console.WriteLine(y); // 20.
```
When y = x happens, the value 10 is copied. After that, x and y are completely independent.<br> Common value types: int, double, bool, char, decimal, struct, enum.<br><br>A reference type variable contains a reference (address) to an object.
```
Person p1 = new Person();
p1.Name = "Alice";
Person p2 = p1;
p2.Name = "Bob";
Console.WriteLine(p1.Name); // Bob
Console.WriteLine(p2.Name); // Bob
```
Both variables still point to the same object.<br>No object was copied. Only the reference was copied.<br> Common reference types: string, arrays, classes, delegates,interfaces

