cards.answer:
Because Point is a struct (value type):<br>The entire contents of p1 are copied into p2 field-by-field.<br>Not a reference. Not an address. A full value copy.<br><br>Even though structs are “value types”, they can still contain references:
```
struct Point{
    public string Label;
}
```
Then: p2 = p1;  results in: p1.Label ──► "A"  p2.Label ──► "A"<br><br>Only the reference inside the struct is copied, not the string itself. So you get: two separate structs but possibly shared referenced objects inside them.<br><br>Struct assignment copies the entire value (all fields), creating a new independent instance; no object identity or shared heap allocation is involved for the struct itself.

