cards.answer:
These two lines differ fundamentally in allocation, object existence, and runtime behavior. Assume
```
class Person
{
    public string Name;
}
```
Person p = null;<br>What happens: No Person object is created, only a reference variable is declared and initialized to “no object”. Only a reference variable exists, pointing to nothing<br><br>Person p = new Person();<br>This triggers full object creation. A heap object is created and p holds its reference.

